data. In the IoBT, one of the most common strategies to enforce the aforementioned tasks involves deploying resourceconstrained spectrum sensors that can monitor and decode transmissions across various radio bands [3]. These sensors offer several benefits, including portability, accuracy, simplicity, and cost-efficiency. However, they are also susceptible to cyberattacks.

On the battlefield, cyberwar and cyberattacks are common hostile activities aimed at infiltrating strategic targets such as enemy communications, area defense, or crucial infrastructures [4]. In this context, IoBT spectrum sensors serve as ideal targets due to their computational and storage limitations, which can impede maintaining up-to-date software and deploying cybersecurity mechanisms. One of the main threats affecting IoBT spectrum sensors is Spectrum Sensing Data Falsification (SSDF) attacks, which main goal is to manipulate the spectrum data that sensors monitor and report. The intentions of SSDF attacks range from concealing illicit transmissions or instigating interference and collisions to simulating non-existent transmissions, thereby misleading enemy communication strategies.

One of the most prominent manners to execute SSDF attacks in IoBT spectrum sensors is to use malware samples, such as backdoors, that get access and manipulate the spectrum data before it is sent. Traditionally, SSDF attacks have been detected by analyzing spectrum data and reaching a consensus between different sensors closely located [5]. However, these detection approaches need redundant and trustworthy sensors and fail when detecting attacks sending outdated spectrum data or adding minor perturbations. Nowadays, some of the most precise and promising solutions combine machine learning (ML) and device fingerprinting [6]. These solutions model the behavior of IoBT spectrum sensors from different perspectives, such as consumption of CPU, RAM, disk, network, or syscalls, to later detect deviations produced by SSDF attacks [7]. However, the effectiveness of existing detection systems has been tested with a limited set of SSDF attacks having a remarkable impact on the device behavior. In this context, there is a lack of work proposing new SSDF attacks able to manipulate spectrum data while impacting the IoBT spectrum sensor behavior in a reduced manner. A rich and heterogeneous set of SSDF attacks is critical to test the suitability of existing detection systems and improve detection systems if needed.

To improve the previous limitations, this work presents two
main contributions. The first is the redesign and implementation of seven SSDF attacks (repeat, mimic, confusion, noise, spoof, freeze, and delay) manipulating the data spectrum while having a reduced impact on IoBT sensors file system. The seven SSDF attacks have been implemented in a physical IoBT spectrum sensor belonging to the ElectroSense platform [8]. Then, the second contribution consists of evaluating and comparing the detection performance of recent and promising detection systems (based on fingerprinting and ML) when IoBT spectrum sensors are affected by the previous seven attacks. The experiments demonstrated that most SSDF attacks remain undetected, with a True Positive Rate in the detection below 0.50 , and more effort is needed from the defensive side.

The remainder of this article is structured as follows. Section II reviews SSDF attacks and detection mechanisms. Section III illustrates the scenario chosen for evaluating the approach novelty and the different SSDF attacks implemented in this work. Section IV describes the attack monitoring and detection results using literature-based methods. Finally, Section V provides the main conclusions of the present work and describes future directions.

