Spectrum sensing is susceptible to a range of malicious activities. According to [13], malevolent actions can be grouped into four distinct categories: (i) misbehavior, (ii) selfishness, (iii) cheating, and (iv) maliciousness, each with distinct attack targets. Misbehavior involves violating rules established by the network or spectrum sensing platform, such as unauthorized transmissions. In contrast, selfishness manifests as monopolizing network resources for the attacker's advantage, for instance, by fabricating non-existent transmissions to disrupt other users. Cheating encompasses the provision of falsified spectrum information, a tactic aimed at improving the attacker's Quality of Service (QoS). Lastly, maliciousness denotes a deliberate attack on spectrum sensing to degrade the QoS of other nodes and diminish network efficiency [14].

The most similar work to the paper at hand regarding attacks and devices is presented in [6]. The authors proposed seven novel SSDF attacks (Repeat, Mimic, Confusion, Noise, Spoof, Freeze, and Delay) affecting spectrum data. All attacks are implemented by modifying the legitimate code of RF spectrum sensors. Additionally, the authors proposed a detection system based on fingerprinting and ML, which provided promising
results when detecting most of the previous attacks. Compared to the paper at hand, during the design and implementation of the SSDF attacks, the authors did not consider their impact on the sensor behavior. Another noteworthy study is [10], where the authors examine frequency distributions of protocol attributes and employ clustering algorithms to distinguish botnet behaviors. They reported an accuracy rate of \(97-100 \%\), mainly relying on network features as behavior markers. Similarly, the research in [9] combines ML techniques with network packets to identify diverse malware affecting IoT devices, achieving a \(95 \%\) accuracy rate on their test datasets. A notable addition to the literature is the work [12]. The authors propose an innovative approach integrating device fingerprinting with ML/DL techniques, specifically Federated Learning (FL). The study concludes that FL models show promising performance in detecting SSDF attacks. However, these models are vulnerable without anti-adversarial mechanisms, especially when more than \(16 \%\) adversaries are present.

Another relevant detection system was presented in [11], proposing a Bayesian-inference-based sliding window trust model for detecting different probabilistic SSDF attacks. However, these studies have a significant limitation in detecting SSDF attacks that repeat identical spectrum data or introduce minor variations. A detailed review of current SSDF attack detection mechanisms, including reputation-based, neighborhood distance, abnormality detection, AI-based, genetic algorithms, and outlier detection approaches, is conducted in [5]. The primary focus o these techniques is the analysis of spectrum data. However, some methods require the collaboration of multiple sensors and operate under the assumption of trusted scenarios.

In conclusion, it is evident that while significant strides have been made in SSDF attacks and detection systems, gaps persist. In particular, there is no work proposing SSDF attacks that affect the RF sensor behavior in a reduced manner. In addition to that, existing detectors have not been evaluated with stealth attacks.

\section*{III. IoBt Spectrum Sensors and SSDF Attacks}

IoBT spectrum sensors are resource-constrained devices, such as Raspberry Pis, equipped with Software-defined Radio (SDR) kits. The primary functions of these sensors involve i) segmenting the RF (Radio Frequency) spectrum into fixedsize intervals; ii) scanning each segment within the entire radio frequency spectrum; iii) collecting various types of data per segment, including IQ Data or Power Spectrum Data (PSD) with information about the spectrum occupancy, and iv) transmitting collected data to a central platform responsible for processing and analyzing the data to provide heterogeneous services.

In this work, the previous functionality is provided by an existing crowdsensing radio frequency platform called ElectroSense [8]. ElectroSense presents a flexible and scalable architecture consisting of three major components: sensors,
a centralized controller infrastructure, and the backend. This work, however, concentrates exclusively on the sensors. Spectrum sensors in ElectroSense are Raspberry Pis 3 Model B equipped with an RTL-SDR USB dongle to monitor and send spectrum data to the backend. The software used on the ElectroSense sensors is written in C++, open source, and publicly available. It starts initiating its components, and then it begins scanning the segments of the radio frequency channels. In each segment, the IQ samples are captured with the RTLSDR USB. These IQ samples, which are quadrature signals in the time domain, are transformed before being sent to the backend. Before the transmission, the SSDF attacks affect the spectrum data by altering the data collection or processing code.

\section*{A. Novel Spectrum Sensing Data Falsification Attacks}

SSDF attacks tamper the data scanned by IoBT spectrum sensors to disrupt the spectrum optimization, monitoring, and decoding services. In particular, this work reimplements seven SSDF attacks proposed in [6] and called repeat, mimic, noise, confusion, spoof, freeze, and delay SSDF attacks. Existing implementations of these attacks use the sensor file system to manipulate spectrum data [6]. However, they strongly affect the sensor behavior and enable their detection [7]. Therefore, this work considers memory variable vectors to reimplement each attack.
1) Repeat: This attack senses one segment of a radio frequency channel and replicates its data across the target spectrum segment range over time. Once the source segment is copied to the auxiliary variable, the data is injected over the whole range until the attack stops. This attack is implemented by Algorithm 1, receiving the attacked frequency as Att_freq and the bandwidth of the attack as Att_bw. The function sdr.readSamples() reads the spectrum samples for a certain frequency. Then, the reading cycle of frequencies (from 80 MHz to 1.6 GHz ) is repeated until the spectrum monitoring task stops.
```
Algorithm 1 SSDF Attack: Repeat
    Repeat_segment ← empty vector
    while Running \(=\) True do
        iq_vector \(=\) sdr.readSamples()
        if Att_freq < current_freq < Att_freq + Att_bw then
            if Repeat_segment = empty then
                Repeat_segment \(=\) iq_vector
            else
                iq_vector \(=\) Repeat_segment
            end if
        end if
    end while
```

2) Freeze: The behavior of the freeze attack is defined as repeating the same sample values of a selected segment over time. It can be seen as a variation of the Repeat attack where different segments are repeated in the same order. Algorithm 2 shows how the selected radio frequency segment
(Freeze_segment) is injected in the segments of the attacked frequency and bandwidth:
```
Algorithm 2 SSDF Attack: Freeze
    Freeze_segment ← empty vector
    SegmentCount ← 0
    while Running = True do
        iq_vector \(=\) sdr.readSamples()
        if Att_freq < current_freq < Att_freq + Att_bw then
            iq_vector \(=\) Freeze_segment \([\) SegmentCount \(]\)
            SegmentCount++
        end if
    end while
```

3) Mimic: The mimic attack is a sophisticated version of the repeat attack. This attack defines a second radio frequency channel segment, and the spectrum values of the second frequency are copied to the first frequency in every monitoring cycle. In contrast to the repeat attack, the spectrum is not just being replaced by the spectrum values at \(t=0\). The implementation details of this attack are provided by Algorithm 3.
```
Algorithm 3 SSDF Attack: Mimic
    Mimic_segment ← empty vector
    \(i \leftarrow 0\)
    while Running \(=\) True do
        iq_vector \(=\) sdr.readSamples()
        if Att_freq < current_freq < Att_freq + Att_bw then
            Mimic_segment append iq_vector
        end if
        if Att_freq \(2<\) current_freq < Att_freq \(2+A t t \_b w\)
    then
            iq_vector \(=\) Mimic_segment \([i]\)
            \(i++\)
        end if
        if current_freq \(>A t t \_f r e q 2+A t t \_b w\) then
            clear Mimic_segment
            Reset \(i \leftarrow 0\)
        end if
    end while
```

4) Spoof: This attack is like the mimic one, where a selection of spectrum segments are replaced by others, but adding noise to the copied segment values. Algorithm 4 shows the implementation of this attack.
5) Noise: It adds random noise to the samples of the attacked radio frequency channel segments. The noise attack implementation uses a system-specific source of randomness, the filesystem path "/dev/urandom". In this sense, the system treats this source as a file. Algorithm 5 shows how noise is added to the iq_vector of the attacked frequencies.
6) Confusion: This SSDF attack swaps the data segments of two selected radio frequency channels. It employs two auxiliary vectors to store the legitimate samples of each target frequency. Then, it swaps the sent data between frequencies and resets the auxiliary vectors. Due to room constraints, its algorithm is not shown.
```
Algorithm 4 SSDF Attack: Spoof
    Spoof_segment ← empty vector
    \(i \leftarrow 0\)
    while Running \(=\) True do
        iq_vector \(=\) sdr.readSamples()
        if Att_freq < current_freq < Att_freq + Att_bw then
            Spoof_segment append iq_vector
        end if
        if Att_freq \(2<\) current_freq \(<A t t_{-} f r e q 2+A t t \_b w\)
    then
            iq_vector \(=\) Spoof_segment \([i]+\) randomValue
            i++
        end if
        if current_freq \(>A t t \_\)freq \(2+A t t \_b w\) then
            clear Spoof_segment
            Reset \(i \leftarrow 0\)
        end if
    end while
```

```
Algorithm 5 SSDF Attack: Noise
    while Running = True do
        iq_vector \(=\) sdr.readSamples()
        if Att_freq < current_freq < Att_freq + Att_bw then
            iq_vector \(=\) iq_vector + randomValue
        end if
    end while
```

7) Delay: From the design perspective, this attack is the most sophisticated one. The values of the affected radio frequency segments need to be stored and repeated after the delay time has been reached. This attack is implemented as a count of segment cycles. It relies on a three-dimensional array consisting of nested vectors containing the iq_vectors for multiple segments over time. Basically, there exist three modes: init, filling, and full. Init is the first iteration, thereafter comes filling, and once the Delay_segment is full, the mode full is activated. The different modes are explained in Algorithm 6 and kept rather simple to demonstrate the idea.

\section*{IV. Detection of SSDF Attacks}

This section evaluates the stealth capabilities of the seven SSDF attacks affecting an ElectroSense sensor and presented in the previous section. For that, recent and promising systems detecting SSDF attacks have been reviewed to later select one of the most effective ones in terms of detection performance. Finally, the selected detector is based on system calls and ML/DL anomaly detection [7].

\section*{A. Detection System based on ML and Fingerprinting}

The selected work uses system calls to create a valuable fingerprint of the sensor behavior and later detect the SSDF attacks. Therefore, the detection system needs to supervise all instructions sent by the sensor scanning process and all its sub-processes to the kernel. This is done with the Linux system monitoring tool perf trace. At this step, only the system calls name, and its corresponding timestamps are saved in a CSV file and used in the subsequent processes.
```
Algorithm 6 SSDF Attack: Delay
    Delay_segment ← empty 3d array
    mode ⟵ init, filling or full
    delay \(\leftarrow x\)
    \(i \leftarrow 0\)
    CurrentIteration ← 0
    while Running \(=\) True do
        iq_vector \(=\) sdr.readSamples ()
        if Att_freq < current_freq < Att_freq + Att_bw then
            if mode \(=\) init then
                Delay_segment append vector of size delay with
    iq_vector at the first position
            end if
            if mode \(==\) filling then
                Delay_segment[i][CurrentIteration] append
    iq_vector
                \(i++\)
            end if
            if mode == full then
                Exchange iq_vector with
    Delay_segment[i][CurrentIteration]
                i++
            end if
        end if
        if current_freq \(>A t t \_\)freq \(+A t t \_b w\) then
            Reset \(i \leftarrow 0\)
            when CurrentIteration \(==\) delay then Reset
    CurrentIteration else CurrentIteration++
        end if
    end while
```


To create such a CSV file, the Raspberry Pi 3, acting as an ElectroSense sensor, was monitored for two and a half hours for every behavior, including normal and under SSDF attacks. It created 300 samples for 30 s of monitoring, inspired by the configuration of [7], where \(360 \times 60 \mathrm{~s}\) was used. For each of the seven SSDF attacks, different configurations of affected data spectrum bandwidth were considered. In particular, the following attacking ranges have been observed: \(20 \mathrm{kHz}, 200 \mathrm{kHz}, 20 \mathrm{MHz}, 200 \mathrm{MHz}\), and 800 MHz . It resulted in 2400 samples of 30 s of monitoring for each bandwidth which meant an approximate total monitoring time of six days, including the preprocessing time.

Once the datasets with normal and malicious system calls were created, they were used in a common ML pipeline. More in detail, the detection framework has been implemented in Python and scikit-learn for preprocessing and machine learning, pickle for object serialization, numpy \& pandas for data frame handling and matplotlib \& seaborn for visualization. The first step of the ML pipeline involves data cleaning to remove erroneous and useless data. To prepare the data for training ML algorithms, the textual data needs to be transformed into numerical data. This is done using a feature extraction process to convert the sequence of system calls into feature vectors. TABLE II describes the nine approaches for feature extraction. Frequency approaches, counting the number of occurrences of the syscalls in a certain time window, have been combined with n-grams up to \(n=3\). The outcome of the feature extraction step is nine datasets, one per feature extraction technique. Two
of the datasets are sequence-related, resulting from applying one-hot encoding and dictionary techniques; the other seven are different configurations of bag-of-words encoded features.

