Finally, Robust covariance, One-Class Support Vector Machine, Isolation Forest, Stochastic Gradient Descent One-Class Support Vector Machine, and the Local Outlier Factor are trained, and their performance is evaluated. For that, the datasets modeling the normal behavior serve as training data for the different ML algorithms. Then, the data of the different configurations of each SSDF attack are evaluated to measure the detection performance.

The performance of the ML models was evaluated based on two different scores, the True Positive Rate (TPR) and the True Negative Rate (TNR). A True Positive (TP) stands for a correctly classified anomaly, while a True Negative (TN) represents a correctly detected normal behavior. The opposite stands for False Positive (FP) and False Negative (FN). Thus, the TPR describes the ratio of actual positives (anomalies) correctly predicted. It is calculated by \(T P R=\frac{T P}{T P+F N}\) Finally, the ratio of actual negatives correctly predicted (normal behavior) is the TNR, calculated by \(T N R=\frac{T N}{T N+F P}\).

After initial experiments, the frequency 3-gram feature obtained the best results. Fig. 1 compares the features for all bandwidths across all ML algorithms. It is worth highlighting the weak performance of the sequence features with dictionary index-encoding and one-hot-encoding. Additionally, the best TPR is reached by the 3-gram frequency feature, and it is the feature extraction method employed in the rest of the evaluation.

