Fig. 3 shows the TPR for the malicious behaviors separately and the average for all the attacks tested across the observed attacking bandwidths. Assessing the differences between the behaviors, spoof, and noise stand out. As the data exploration has yielded, the system calls bash open and bash \({ }_{\text {close }}\) appear, as those attacks generate deviating system calls due to their dependence on a file to generate random noise. The Robust Covariance algorithm detects those anomalies in the lower bandwidths perfectly with 1 TPR. Attacks that involve two segments, mimic, confusion, and spoof, have an increased detection rate at the 800 MHz bandwidth, possibly because almost every data point needs to be manipulated.

In conclusion, most SSDF attacks present a low TPR, with an average lower than 0.50 for all ML models and frequency bandwidths. These are poor detection results compared to the literature, where most attacks are almost perfectly detected [12], [6]. Only repeat and freeze attacks are not ideally detected in the literature. Still, with the new implementation, these attacks have an even lower TPR, with a TPR below 0.20 in most model/bandwidth combinations. Therefore, it can be stated that the new implementations offer better resilience against state-of-the-art detection methods, showing their weaknesses when stealth implementations are present.

\section*{V. Conclusion and Future Work}

This work has designed and implemented novel versions of seven existing SSDF attacks to test the robustness of existing detection systems. These SSDF attacks manipulate the data spectrum in the sensor memory to achieve a low impact on the sensor behavior, something not considered in the literature. The seven attacks have been deployed in a Raspberry Pi 3, acting as a spectrum sensor of an open-source and crowdsensing platform called ElectroSense. After that, a recent

